body:
@GeorgePetri Installation via platform package managers - like `apt` in Ubuntu - comes with its own issues. We trust these managers will put files where they belong, and won't mess up the system, right?

The shells themselves have package managers, created to help users install plugins and add-ons, with the same level of ease and confidence you use `apt`. These add-on managers know where to place files, and automate the entire process of searching, installing, updating and removing shell plugins. This is pretty much the 'standard' way of adding extensions to your shell, and from the end-user's perspective, anything outside of that will be 'extra work'. 

IMHO, supporting these shell add-on managers would be preferable, because they are **cross-platform**. Also because many of them make it easier for your package to be discovered - i.e., via built-in search functions that provide descriptions and links (like Oh-My-Fish).

@luxlogica I like to avoid a shell package manager. If you don't have many shell add-ons for example I only use starship because the fish shell since is nicely configured by default, a shell package manager is only extra work. If I want to upgrade my system, I need to use apt, AND Oh-My-Fish which add extra friction for no reason.

However I think supporting shell package managers has some benefit for users that like them, that use many add-ons. I just don't think it should be the 'standard' as you put it way to add extensions to your shell.

I am in favour of system package managers with interactive install prompt like the 3rd option from #47.
